#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:1
msgid "Derivation Outputs and Types of Derivations"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:3
msgid ""
"As stated on the [main pages on derivations](../index.md#store-derivation), "
"a derivation produces [store objects](../../../store/store-object.md), which "
"are known as the _outputs_ of the derivation. Indeed, the entire point of "
"derivations is to produce these outputs, and to reliably and reproducibly "
"produce these derivations each time the derivation is run."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:7
msgid ""
"One of the parts of a derivation is its _outputs specification_, which "
"specifies certain information about the outputs the derivation produces when "
"run. The outputs specification is a map, from names to specifications for "
"individual outputs."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:10
msgid "Output Names"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:12
msgid ""
"Output names can be any string which is also a valid [store path name]"
"(../../../store/store-path.md#name). The name mapped to each output "
"specification is not actually the name of the output. In the general case, "
"the output store object has name `derivationName + \"-\" + outputSpecName`, "
"not any other metadata about it. However, an output spec named \"out\" "
"describes and output store object whose name is just the derivation name."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:17
msgid "**Example**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:19
msgid "A derivation is named `hello`, and has two outputs, `out`, and `dev`"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:21
msgid "The derivation's path will be: `/nix/store/<hash>-hello.drv`."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:23
msgid "The store path of `out` will be: `/nix/store/<hash>-hello`."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:25
msgid "The store path of `dev` will be: `/nix/store/<hash>-hello-dev`."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:27
msgid ""
"The outputs are the derivations are the [store objects](../../../store/store-"
"object.md) it is obligated to produce."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:29
msgid "**Note**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:31
msgid ""
"The formal terminology here is somewhat at odds with everyday communication "
"in the Nix community today. \"output\" in casual usage tends to refer to "
"either to the actual output store object, or the notional output spec, "
"depending on context."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:34
msgid ""
"For example \"hello's `dev` output\" means the store object referred to by "
"the store path `/nix/store/<hash>-hello-dev`. It is unusual to call this the "
"\"`hello-dev` output\", even though `hello-dev` is the actual name of that "
"store object."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:37
msgid "Types of output addressing"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:39
msgid ""
"The main information contained in an output specification is how the "
"derivation output is addressed. In particular, the specification decides:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:42
msgid ""
"whether the output is [content-addressed](./content-address.md) or [input-"
"addressed](./input-address.md)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:44
msgid "if the content is content-addressed, how is it content addressed"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:46
msgid ""
"if the content is content-addressed, [what is its content address](./content-"
"address.md#fixed) (and thus what is its \\[store path\\])"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:48
msgid "Output checks"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:50
msgid ""
"In addition to the output specification above, a derivation may mandate "
"additional _checks_ on its outputs: properties that must hold for the "
"produced store objects in order for the build to be considered successful. "
"Checks may be specified separately for each output, or once for all outputs "
"of the derivation."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:54
msgid "Reference checks"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:56
msgid ""
"The main checks constrain the [references](../../../glossary.md#gloss-"
"reference) of an output. These checks vary along two axes, yielding 4 "
"possible checks:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:59
msgid ""
"Whether the check applies to the _direct_ references of the output, or to "
"its entire [closure](../../../glossary.md#gloss-closure) via _transitive_ "
"references ([requisites](../../../store/store-object.md#references))."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:61
msgid ""
"Whether the check _allows_ a set of references (every reference must be a "
"member of the set, though not every member needs to be referenced), or "
"_disallows_ a set of references (no reference may be a member of the set)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:63
msgid "The four checks are:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:65
msgid ""
"\\[_allowed references_\\]{#allowed-references}: An optional set; the "
"output's references must be a subset of it. When the set is absent the check "
"is skipped, i.e. every reference is allowed."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:69
msgid ""
"For example, the empty set enforces that the output has no runtime "
"dependencies at all."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:71
msgid "**Usage note**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:73
msgid ""
"This is used in NixOS to check that generated files such as initial ramdisks "
"for booting Linux don't have accidental dependencies on other paths in the "
"Nix store."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:75
msgid ""
"\\[_allowed requisites_\\]{#allowed-requisites}: Like _allowed references_, "
"but applying to the whole closure of the output: every transitive dependency "
"must be a member of the set."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:79
msgid ""
"\\[_disallowed references_\\]{#disallowed-references}: A set of which no "
"member may be a direct reference of the output. (There is no need for this "
"check to be optional: disallowing nothing is the same as skipping the check.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:83
msgid ""
"\\[_disallowed requisites_\\]{#disallowed-requisites}: Like _disallowed "
"references_, but applying to the whole closure of the output: no member of "
"the set may appear anywhere in the output's transitive dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:87
msgid ""
"The references of a store object are always store paths. However, if every "
"element of these sets had to be a store path, it would be hard-to-impossible "
"to constrain references from outputs _to other outputs_ of the same "
"derivation, because in general the store paths of outputs are not known "
"until the derivation is built. For this reason, an element of these sets may "
"also be an _output name_ of the derivation being checked, standing for the "
"store path of that output, whatever it turns out to be. For example, an "
"output can be permitted to reference itself by including its own name in its "
"_allowed references_."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:92
msgid "Size checks"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:94
msgid ""
"\\[_max size_\\]{#max-size}: The [NAR size](../../../store/store-object."
"md#nar-size) of the output's store object may not exceed the given number of "
"bytes."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:97
msgid ""
"\\[_max closure size_\\]{#max-closure-size}: The [closure NAR size](../../../"
"store/store-object.md#closure-nar-size) of the output's store object may not "
"exceed the given number of bytes."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:100
msgid "Self-reference handling"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:102
msgid ""
"\\[_ignore self references_\\]{#ignore-self-refs}: Whether references of an "
"output to itself are exempted from the reference checks above."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:105
msgid "Reference scanning"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:107
msgid ""
"Besides the output checks, there is one more per-output setting. It is _not_ "
"a check — nothing is verified about the produced store object — but it is "
"configured in the same per-output manner, so it is documented here:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:110
msgid ""
"\\[_unsafe discard references_\\]{#unsafe-discard-references}: Disables "
"[scanning the output for run-time references](../../building.md#scanning-for-"
"references) altogether. The output is then registered as having no "
"references, regardless of its contents."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:114
msgid ""
"This is useful, for example, when generating self-contained filesystem "
"images with their own embedded Nix store: hashes found inside such an image "
"refer to the embedded store and not to the host's Nix store."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:117
msgid ""
"As the name suggests, this is unsafe: discarding references that are in fact "
"needed at run time makes the closure incomplete, so copying the output "
"elsewhere will not bring its dependencies along."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:125
msgid "Types of derivations"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:127
msgid ""
"The sections on each type of derivation output addressing ended up "
"discussing other attributes of the derivation besides its outputs, such as "
"purity, scheduling, determinism, etc. This is no concidence; for the type of "
"a derivation is in fact one-for-one with the type of its outputs:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:130
msgid ""
"A derivation that produces _xyz-addressed_ outputs is an _xyz-addressing_ "
"derivations."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:132
msgid "The rules for this are fairly concise:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:134
msgid "All the outputs must be of the same type / use the same addressing"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:136
msgid "The derivation must have at least one output"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:138
msgid ""
"Additionally, if the outputs are fixed content-addressed, there must be "
"exactly one output, whose specification is mapped from the name `out`. (The "
"name `out` is special, according to the rules described above. Having only "
"one output and calling its specification `out` means the single output is "
"effectively anonymous; the store path just has the derivation name.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:142
msgid "(This is an arbitrary restriction that could be lifted.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:144
msgid ""
"The output is either _fixed_ or _floating_, indicating whether the store "
"path is known prior to building it."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:146
msgid "With fixed content-addressing it is fixed."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:148
msgid ""
"A _fixed content-addressing_ derivation is also called a _fixed-output "
"derivation_, since that is the only currently-implemented form of fixed-"
"output addressing"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:150
msgid "With floating content-addressing or input-addressing it is floating."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:152
msgid ""
"Thus, historically with Nix, with no experimental features enabled, _all_ "
"outputs are fixed."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:154
msgid ""
"The derivation may be _pure_ or _impure_, indicating what read access to the "
"outside world the [builder](../index.md#builder) has."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:156
msgid "An input-addressing derivation _must_ be pure."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:158
msgid ""
"If it is impure, we would have a large problem, because an input-addressed "
"derivation always produces outputs with the same paths."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:161
msgid "A content-addressing derivation may be pure or impure"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:163
msgid ""
"If it is impure, it may be fixed (typical), or it may be floating if the "
"additional [`impure-derivations`](../../../development/experimental-features."
"md#xp-feature-impure-derivations) experimental feature is enabled."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:165
msgid "If it is pure, it must be floating."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:167
msgid "Pure, fixed content-addressing derivations are not supported"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:169
msgid ""
"There is no use for this forth combination. The sole purpose of an output's "
"store path being fixed is to support the derivation being impure."
msgstr ""
